WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.630
- As a consultant you spend a lot

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:04.120
of time in interviews with clients.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.540
So, you sit together
with clients in meets,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.520
ask them questions, and pretty much want

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.650
to extract information from them.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.280
The same is, of course, true
for corporate professionals.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.480
You will often sit
together with colleagues,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.470
ask them questions potentially
from other departments,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.340
try to learn from their role, and try

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.970
to get information that
is relevant to your job.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.080
This is exactly the topic of today.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.900
So, welcome to another coffee break

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.940
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:30.990
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.960
I'm a former McKinsey
consultant and project lead.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.230
And in this video I'm going
to share my top five tips

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.520
of how to conduct great
and professional interviews

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.720
based on my own experience in consulting.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.360
So, without further ado let's get

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.860
started with the five tips.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.730
And tip number one is
to strategically think

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.870
about who is scheduling the meeting?

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.920
As a young consultant
maybe you're a bit naive,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.210
as for sure I was, so
you might just think,

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.310
oh, okay, I want to have
an interview with this guy.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.310
Let me just hit him up
and send him an invite.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.920
And while this might work
perfectly fine in many cases,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.910
what might happen to you
is that you're facing

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.870
some kind of political situation,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.040
where then the client if
he just sees an invite

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.113
from a junior consultant maybe tells you,

00:01:10.113 --> 00:01:13.130
ah, no, you know, this week
my schedule is so busy.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.270
Maybe next week we can find a spot,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.600
maybe 30 minutes end of next week.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.420
But this can often be
quite difficult for you.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.210
Because one week can be a
very long amount of time

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.340
in the context of a project,
where maybe the whole project

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.160
only goes six, seven, eight weeks.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.160
So, my advance again
would be to think about

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.130
who sends the invite, who
is asking for the meeting.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.460
Does it really need to be you?

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.460
Or is there maybe a
way that the supervisor

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.400
of the person you want to talk to asks

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.180
for the meeting, invites the person?

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.590
Because, of course, this often puts

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.510
the meeting much higher on the agenda.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.670
The importance is of
much more significance

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.510
if this is the way the
meeting is scheduled

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.200
than if you are the one asking for it.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.170
Tip number two, before you walk into

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.520
such a meeting or such
an interview situation

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.530
ask yourself, what would success mean?

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.880
So, after the meeting,
what would you know now?

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.960
What you have received from the client

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.402
for the meeting to be a success for you?

00:02:02.402 --> 00:02:03.930
The worst thing that can
happen in these situations

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.100
is that you walk into
a meeting unprepared,

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.630
maybe just have a broad topic

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.410
in your mind that you want to talk about.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.110
But the meeting pretty much
lacks a lot of structure.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.600
And afterwards all types of questions come

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.840
to your mind that you
would have liked to ask,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.000
but someone forgot to ask in the moment.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.130
In addition to that, of
course, coming unprepared

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.420
to such a meeting can
also seem unprofessional.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.940
And in a political situation
where you're maybe facing

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.730
a hostile client he might
also call you out for that.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.800
And then maybe afterwards he walks across

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.830
the organization and
talks to his colleagues,

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.130
maybe his teammate, or
maybe even a board member

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.650
that the consultants from
this and this consulting firm

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.791
are unprofessional, were unprepared

00:02:39.791 --> 00:02:40.730
when conducting the interview with them.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:41.860
Of course, this is an impression

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.410
that you will always want to avoid.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.500
This will make you look
bad as an associate,

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.800
but will also make your whole
consulting firm look bad.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.630
So make sure that you're well-prepared,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.940
write down the questions
that you want to ask.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.820
Think about a structure for the interview

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.800
that you want to conduct.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.050
And if you want to learn more
about this I recently made,

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:00.190
also, a video about
note-taking, how to write down

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.280
these insights that you
get from the interview.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.430
If you want to learn more
about it check out the video.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.100
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.980
Tip number three is to be
empathetic with your client

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.580
and focus on listening and not talking.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.260
Many young consultants seem to tend

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.510
towards talk a lot in these meetings.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.511
You want to prove that you
know what you're talking about.

00:03:18.511 --> 00:03:20.000
You want to prove that you're competent.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.730
So when you observe rather
unexperienced people

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.900
often you see that they try to talk a lot.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.800
They try to get a lot of
airtime in these settings.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.010
What is much better, though,

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.030
and one thing that is emphasized in many,

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.170
many onboarding training programs

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.240
of these consulting firms is to focus

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.130
on letting the client speak,

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:39.610
and see yourself rather
in a listening role.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.880
In the end you want
information from the client.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.420
This is what in most cases will

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.910
really help you and the project.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.960
As a rule of thumb for
these interviews try

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.700
to restrict your speaking share to 20%.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:53.330
So you talk 20%, the client talks 80%.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.930
Focus on listening.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.960
But when you listen don't just sit there

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.980
and try to put on a poker face.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.200
But try to engage in active listening.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.110
So whenever the client says something try

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.990
to occasionally say,
okay, yes, understood,

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.333
understood, thank you,
mm-hmm, mm-hmm, okay.

00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:11.270
Give them the impression that
you are actively listening,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:14.090
that you're actively
processing what he is saying.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.710
This would often
encourage them to give you

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.460
even more information, to talk more.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.190
And of course, these are the ingredients

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.340
of a successful interview.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.130
And, of course, highly related to that

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.170
is the importance of being empathetic.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:26.110
Try to really reach your client.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.490
If he is telling you something,

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.480
if he is saying something to
you, what are his feelings?

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.770
Is he comfortable, is he
uncomfortable with the situation?

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.840
Is he starting to become defensive?

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.500
Is this is a topic he's
super enthusiastic about?

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.610
He might even want to share
even more with you on.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:44.100
Reading the room in this
way is really crucial

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.150
to get a feeling for also
all the political situations,

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:50.400
which are always so important
in corporate contexts.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.920
There's pretty much no
thing that is as important

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.550
as empathy when interacting with clients.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.600
This is also something you can learn.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.090
It's a skill that you can develop.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.290
To focus on that start to become sensitive

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.550
for these kinds of topics.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.710
Now, tip number four,
this is a little tactic

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.950
that you can do in the
middle of an interview.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.940
And this is what I learned from a project

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:11.160
lead I worked together with.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.570
We were both sitting in the
interview with a client.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.170
We were asking the client
a couple of things.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.500
And he then said something,

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.410
gave us a short and brief answer.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.680
But it was clear that we
needed much more from him.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.520
And he apparently wasn't really willing

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.900
to just give it all out,
just give it all away.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.170
So, what I observed my project lead doing

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.800
is that after we got a
little bit of information

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.040
he didn't just ask one
question after the other

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:34.870
trying to ask for it, trying to fish

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.020
for the other things
that he wanted to hear.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.900
But he was just pretty much looking

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.063
at the client and saying nothing.

00:05:49.577 --> 00:05:51.210
And he was doing that
for quite a long time,

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.557
up to the moment when it
really felt uncomfortable.

00:05:54.557 --> 00:05:56.920
And this is really a
tactic that you can employ

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.650
for your conversations as well.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.410
People often feel very
uncomfortable in moments of silence.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.700
People will have the urge to break

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.760
the silence, to say something,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.460
to provide you with
additional information.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:11.690
Of course, it's important to be

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.350
a bit careful with this, don't overdo it.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.490
But try this out occasionally.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.730
And in the right moments this
can be a little technique

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.410
that really makes the difference
and can suddenly open up

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.610
a client who wasn't
really willing and open

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.540
to share information with
you in the beginning.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.120
So, make use of this power of silence.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.880
And, also, leave me a
comment if you've done

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.430
something similar in the past.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:33.830
Let me know how this worked out.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:36.000
My fifth tip is to
always write a follow-up,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.170
always write meeting minutes
after these conversations.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.070
Often, in these meetings
in the end you agree

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:43.480
on a couple of things, maybe
to-dos that you are doing,

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:46.280
or the client is doing, or
some other types of next steps.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.710
But, then, what I have
really frequently seeing

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.360
is that then maybe if you do not put

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.700
this in writing, then a week passes.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:55.130
Maybe a week later nothing has happened.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.470
And then if you talk to
the client and confront him

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.394
on something that he wanted to do,

00:06:59.394 --> 00:07:01.110
then you will often see
that he did not do that.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.600
He might also frame that
as a misunderstanding,

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.040
that maybe it wasn't really clear to him

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.430
that this is really something
that you expected him to do.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.660
So just make it a habit
of yours that pretty much

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.310
always after these meetings,
after these interviews,

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.050
you just write a very short email.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.670
Just make it bullet point format.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.420
Just write down what you agreed on,

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.340
what you discussed, what the results were.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.220
And, especially, also specific to-dos

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.370
if either you or the
client agree to something.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.300
You will quickly see that
this is a habit that makes

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:27.960
your whole client relationship
much more reliable.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.970
This is also a technique that you can use

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.990
to build trust with your client.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.920
There is a very helpful framework

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.430
that is often taught
in executive education

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.590
and also consulting onboarding programs.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.690
And this is called the trust formula.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:42.740
And I don't want to go
into too much detail now.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.390
Because it will be a
topic for a future video.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.490
But pretty much what it comes
down to is that creating

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.257
these moments where
you agree on something,

00:07:49.257 --> 00:07:51.210
and the client agrees on something,

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.920
and then both of you deliver
against these promises,

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.660
these often create
opportunities to create trust.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.790
This is how trust is formed and created.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:01.681
This is how you can build, and sustain,

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:03.000
and deepen your client relationship.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.110
I hope you found these
tips helpful and applicable

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.520
to your work reality as well, independent

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:10.060
whether you work in consulting,
industry, or banking.

